<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Epiphany</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Theophany, Three Kings' Day, Little Christmas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Epiphany, known as Theophany in Eastern Christian tradition, is a Christian feast day commemorating the visit of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magi">Magi</a>, the baptism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> and the wedding at Cana, traditionally on 6 January. It may have originated in the Greek-speaking east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> as a feast of the baptism of Jesus; the earliest reference to it as a Christian feast, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a>, dates from 361. Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> principally commemorates the Magi's visit to the Christ Child, seen as his manifestation to the Gentiles, while Eastern Christians commemorate his baptism in the River Jordan. Eastern Churches that follow the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julian-calendar">Julian calendar</a> observe the feast on 19 January of the Gregorian calendar.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/epiphany-holiday.jpg" alt="Epiphany" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gentile da Fabriano · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epifanía_Gentile_02.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Part of the Eastern Christian churches keep Christmas on 25 December of the Julian calendar, which currently corresponds to 7 January, and the Armenian Christmas falls on 6 January, the traditional day of <b>Epiphany</b>.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Epiphany</b>, on 6 January, may have originated in the Greek-speaking east of the empire as a feast of the baptism of Jesus; the earliest reference to it as a Christian feast, by Ammianus Marcellinus, dates from 361, and Western Christianity principally commemorates the visit of the Magi, Eastern…</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li><li><blockquote>The twelve days are those of the Christmas season, from Christmas Day to the day before <b>Epiphany</b> on 6 January.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-twelve-days-of-christmas">The Twelve Days of Christmas</a></li><li><blockquote>By tradition Joan of Arc was born on <b>Epiphany</b> in the village of Domrémy, in north-eastern France.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-6">6 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
